  "notes": "\"Nashville's reluctant producer,\" and a songwriter first — \"song and production are one decision.\" The house grammar he built is quoted: \"a hard-backbeat two-beat with quarter kick for the dance cuts and cross-stick-into-full-kit for the ballads,\" and the bass rule is stated as a rule: \"live bass in quarter-note root motion on the boogies, tied whole notes on the ballads, doubling the kick on beat 1 as a rule.\" Harmony is \"I–IV–V plus vi–IV–I–V on the ballads and a I–♭VII–IV rock borrow on the uptempo cuts.\" The identifiable production choice is a vocal-arrangement rule and it is the counter parameter here: \"harmony vocal as the hook mechanism: the second voice enters in parallel thirds at the chorus and vacates the verses entirely\" — so the counter is an answer that appears rather than a pad that runs, which is why `densityRatio` is mid-high against a `fillGapsOnly` inherited true. The twin-lead palette adds \"Telecaster and pedal steel trading 2-bar answers with fiddle doubling the steel a third above on choruses.\" ⚠ Boundary note carried from the research: \"the boogie and slow-swing grooves in this catalogue belong to the sibling archetype genres; his arena midtempos are the part modelled here.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: \"roster, chart record and songwriter-first orientation documented; technical method not [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
